Dafny's verifier checks your method body against its contract statically,
before anything runs: every precondition must be provably true at each call
site, every loop invariant must hold on entry and be re-established by every
branch of the loop body, and every postcondition must follow from the state
on exit. A verification failure means the verifier could not prove one of
these obligations as written — it does not necessarily mean the strategy's
logic is wrong, only that the proof wasn't there yet.

